"CONSUMER CONTRACTS\ndeciding how to classify them, this Restatement looked to the component of the agreement that\nwas the focus of the dispute.)\nf Clickwrap. In electronic and web-based transactions, assent is often manifested by\nclicking an \"I Agree\" button. That procedure is the digital equivalent of a signature at the bottom\nof a printed form. See UNIF. ELECTRONIC TRANSACTIONS ACT § 7 (UNIF. L. COMM'N 1999). The\n\"I Agree\" button commonly appears below a scroll-down window that contains the standard terms.\nSee, e.g., Caspi v. Microsoft Network, LLC, 732 A.2d 528, 532 (N.J. Super. Ct. App. Div. 1999)\n(finding no violation of the public policy of New Jersey in enforcing the membership agreement\nfor an online computer service in which prospective subscribers were required to click either\n\"I agree\" or \"I don't agree\" to terms); Forrest v. Verizon Commc'ns, Inc., 805 A.2d 1007, 1010\n(D.C. 2002) (enforcing a forum-selection clause in a contract that subscriber entered by clicking\nan \"Accept\" button below the scroll box); Moore v. Microsoft Corp., 741 N.Y.S.2d 91, 92 (N.Y.\nApp. Div. 2002) (ruling that a validly binding contract was formed when user indicated assent to\nthe end-user license agreement by clicking on an icon before proceeding with the download of the\nsoftware). In some cases, the \"I Agree\" button appears next to a link that would take the consumer\nto another page with the standard terms. See, e.g., Comb v. PayPal, Inc., 218 F. Supp. 2d 1165\n(N.D. Cal. 2002) (classifying business's user agreement, in which a link to the text of the terms is\nat the bottom of the application, as a clickwrap contract); Swift v. Zynga Game Network, Inc., 805\nF. Supp. 2d 904 (N.D. Cal. 2011) (granting a motion to compel arbitration when a clickwrap\npresentation with hyperlinked terms provided the user notice and opportunity to review the terms\nof service prior to acceptance); Fteja v. Facebook, 841 F. Supp. 2d 829 (S.D.N.Y. 2012) (ruling\nthat a user assented to business's terms of use even though they were only available through a\nhyperlink). Those \"clickwrap,\" \"sign-in-wrap\" (when the active manifestation to the transaction\nand the terms occur simultaneously, upon the click of a \"sign in\" or \"buy now\" button, next to a\nnotice and a link to the terms), or \"scrollwrap\" (when the terms appear in a box above the \"I Agree\"\nbutton) terms are adopted, as long as the manner in which terms and notice of terms are presented\nsatisfies the notice requirements that focus on language, placement, and other aspects that enhance\nthe conspicuousness of the terms. See, e.g., Berkson v. Gogo LLC, 97 F. Supp. 3d 359 (E.D.N.Y.\n2015); Corwin v. NYC Bike Share, LLC, 238 F. Supp. 3d 475 (S.D.N.Y. 2017) (explaining how\nvarious forms of presenting terms online, regardless of the particular details, must meet the\nconstructive-notice requirement); Barbanell v. One Med. Group, No. CGC-18-566232, 2019 Cal.\nSuper. LEXIS 1411, at *13 (Cal. Super. Ct. Apr. 3, 2019) (explaining that reasonable notice and a\nmanifestation of assent are necessary for the formation of an agreement regardless of how such\nagreement is presented, and noting that \"simply because an internet agreement is classified as a\n'Clickwrap' agreement does not mandate that it is valid\"). They are adopted under subsection (a)\nof this Section.\nAt times, the notice adjacent to the \"click\" refers to additional terms, available on a\ndifferent website. These referenced terms are also adopted, as long as they are reasonably\naccessible by the consumer. See DeJohn v. TV Corp. Int'l, 245 F. Supp. 2d 913 (C.D. Ill. 2003)\n(finding that consumer was bound by the terms of an agreement, part of which were incorporated\n60\n@§2\n"